Maki, the owner's pager, the second of the owner's programs on Zenix (roadmap block 3: the text-UI library, then
Sumi, Maki, Markdown; DESIGN D27 section 9; docs/d27-c-library.md, "Maki and Markdown on Zenix"; the user's page is
docs/man/maki.txt)

What it is: a pager in the way of less that draws in the terminal (colours, search, pipes, directories, follow, big
files never read whole), written for AmigaOS over tui.library. On Zenix it is /bin/maki, a program with shared code
(ZEX2: a second Maki costs only its data), linked with the text-UI library's pure build (build/libtui-pure.a,
ports/tui) and the C library's (build/libc-pure.a). Zenix's less and more stay: Maki is one more pager, not their
replacement.

upstream/  the owner's source, one source for AmigaOS and Zenix (D27 9, rule 1; owner 2026-10-28), pinned here and
           never changed: the owner's TextTools repository (public, MIT), commit
           ff34b48dea52ab22f23384d5a5829e33e2caef5a (2026-10-30), Maki 0.1b7: src/*.c and src/*.h, README.md as README
           (the user's documentation, which docs/man/maki.txt follows) and LICENSE (MIT, the notice that travels with
           the program: tools/mkroot.sh and the package maki put it in /usr/share/licenses/maki, with the text-UI
           library's as maki-tui).

Zenix's layer is ports/common (GPL-2.0-only, shared with Sumi and Markdown): AmigaOS's names over the C library; the
names Maki maps are "Maki" (ENVARC:Maki, ENV:Maki) and "FileColours" (ENVARC:FileColours, ENV:FileColours: the
colours of a directory's names, shared on AmigaOS with ls and Ahiru). What each call does and the choices are in
ports/common/README; the ones Maki is the first to need: "*" as /dev/tty (the keys while a pipe is read), its read
packets on a pipe (SendPkt/GetMsg) and their reply port's signal bit for the text-UI library's wait, the AmigaOS
protection bits from the Unix mode (a directory's listing), DateToStr, GetVar for MAKI and EDITOR, SystemTags with
SYS_Input/SYS_Output (v: the editor in the terminal).

Maki's own (for the main session to confirm with the layer's choices):
- The stack: STACK_DEFAULT (16 KB): -fstack-usage gives smain 1.3 KB the largest frame, the rest under 0.5 KB; the one
  recursion is the regular expressions' (Sumi's, 60 bytes a level, at most one per repeat in a 128-byte pattern).
- utf8.library (composing an accent written apart onto its letter) never opens: such accents are left out, as upstream
  does without the library.

Found in upstream (for the owner's repository, not changed here):
- Memory: about 202 KB of data while one file is open on 80 x 32 (ps); 650 KB with Maki 0.1b6 and tui.library
  0.5b1 (the cells of a 40,000-character line, its bytes and an eight-slot file cache made at the start, and 16-byte
  cells). Since 0.1b7 the line's cells and bytes and the cache's slots grow when needed, and since tui.library 0.5b3
  a cell is 6 bytes.
- Stopping a search (stopped()) reads only the Ctrl-C signal (SetSignal); where ^C is a key (Zenix's raw mode, the
  text-UI library's keys) nothing stops a long search; a look for a key (TuiKeyWaiting) would do it on both.
- The pipe's reader looks into AmigaOS's FileHandle (fh_Type, fh_Buf, fh_Pos, fh_End, fh_Arg1) and sends packets
  itself: the layer has to imitate those structures (amigados.h); a pipe read of the OS layer's (one call) would be
  simpler to give on another system.
- The default editor is "Sumi" (capital S): AmigaOS finds the command whatever its case; on a system whose names keep
  their case it is "sumi" (the layer maps it).
- Messages and help keep AmigaOS words ("ENVARC:Maki/Maki.keys", ENV:EDITOR), as Sumi's do.

Tests: tools/maki-test.sh (FS-UAE, the stock A1200; in make check): maki opens a file, the grid is read back with
termtest screen (CON_READ), keys are put in (CON_INPUT): a page on, a search, quit; a pipe with a late line, a
directory, the output into a pipe; the settings; the memory printed.

To update: tools/texttools-vendor.sh maki /path/to/TextTools [COMMIT] (the files above from that commit, unchanged),
put the commit here, build, run tools/maki-test.sh, and bring docs/man/maki.txt in step with the README's changes; if
upstream starts to use an AmigaOS call amigados.h does not have, the build says so (an undeclared function):
ports/common gets it.
